WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.624
<v Peter>Same onion, same knife.

2
00:00:02.624 --> 00:00:04.600
<v Peter>The difference is the hands.

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.929
<v Peter>Today: how to hold the knife, how to protect your fingers – and the classic cuts,

4
00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:14.640
<v Peter>from julienne to brunoise.

5
00:00:18.235 --> 00:00:19.260
<v Peter>Hi, I'm Peter.

6
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.385
<v Peter>Susanne showed you the tools.

7
00:00:21.385 --> 00:00:26.000
<v Peter>Today I'll show you how to use them – without cutting yourself.

8
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:33.071
<v Peter>Knives send over four hundred thousand people a year to emergency rooms in the US

9
00:00:33.071 --> 00:00:33.770
<v Peter>alone.

10
00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:37.000
<v Peter>Two thirds of it: fingers and thumbs.

11
00:00:37.501 --> 00:00:41.762
<v Peter>Almost always the same story: the knife slipped.

12
00:00:41.762 --> 00:00:47.000
<v Peter>A dull blade, a wobbly board, or a hand in the wrong place.

13
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:48.288
<v Peter>Five rules.

14
00:00:48.288 --> 00:00:50.510
<v Peter>A sharp knife – dull ones slip.

15
00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:54.022
<v Peter>A board that can't move: a damp cloth underneath.

16
00:00:54.022 --> 00:00:56.388
<v Peter>Flat side down, so nothing rolls.

17
00:00:56.388 --> 00:00:59.900
<v Peter>Cut away from your body, never towards your hand.

18
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.265
<v Peter>And if a knife falls – step back.

19
00:01:02.265 --> 00:01:03.340
<v Peter>Never catch it.

20
00:01:06.377 --> 00:01:07.934
<v Peter>First, the knife hand.

21
00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:12.816
<v Peter>Pinch the blade right in front of the handle – thumb and index finger

22
00:01:12.816 --> 00:01:16.000
<v Peter>– and wrap the other three around the handle.

23
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:17.739
<v Peter>That's the pinch grip.

24
00:01:17.739 --> 00:01:20.497
<v Peter>Control comes from the blade, not from the handle

25
00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:22.580
<v Peter>– the knife can't twist in your hand.

26
00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:29.069
<v Peter>Now the other hand – the one that matters most.

27
00:01:29.069 --> 00:01:32.558
<v Peter>Curl your fingertips under, like a claw.

28
00:01:32.558 --> 00:01:35.000
<v Peter>The knuckles face the blade.

29
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:40.438
<v Peter>The flat of the blade rides against your knuckles – it can't reach your fingertips.

30
00:01:40.438 --> 00:01:42.640
<v Peter>Your thumb stays behind your fingers.

31
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.496
<v Peter>After every slice, the claw walks back a little.

32
00:01:45.496 --> 00:01:47.340
<v Peter>The knuckles set the thickness.

33
00:01:48.758 --> 00:01:51.945
<v Peter>Look at your knuckles, not at the blade.

34
00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:58.000
<v Peter>If the knife never rises above the knuckles, it can never find your fingers.

35
00:01:58.726 --> 00:02:03.253
<v Peter>Every classic cut starts the same way: make it flat.

36
00:02:03.253 --> 00:02:04.298
<v Peter>Then slices.

37
00:02:04.298 --> 00:02:05.343
<v Peter>Then sticks.

38
00:02:05.343 --> 00:02:06.214
<v Peter>Then dice.

39
00:02:06.214 --> 00:02:09.000
<v Peter>The first step decides the rest.

40
00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.370
<v Peter>Take a thin slice off the round side – now it sits flat.

41
00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:16.825
<v Peter>Cut it into slices as thick as you want your dice.

42
00:02:16.825 --> 00:02:18.829
<v Peter>Stack the slices, cut sticks.

43
00:02:18.829 --> 00:02:21.455
<v Peter>Line up the sticks, cut across – dice.

44
00:02:21.455 --> 00:02:25.740
<v Peter>Same thickness at every step, and every cube is the same size.

45
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.051
<v Peter>Why the fuss about size?

46
00:02:29.051 --> 00:02:31.700
<v Peter>Same-sized pieces cook at the same speed.

47
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.995
<v Peter>Small dice for a sauce, big rough chunks – mirepoix

48
00:02:34.995 --> 00:02:37.580
<v Peter>– for a stock that gets strained anyway.

49
00:02:40.134 --> 00:02:41.778
<v Peter>Julienne: matchsticks.

50
00:02:41.778 --> 00:02:45.142
<v Peter>Two millimetres thick, five centimetres long.

51
00:02:45.142 --> 00:02:50.000
<v Peter>Cut those across and you get brunoise – the finest dice there is.

52
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.330
<v Peter>Five-centimetre pieces, flat side down.

53
00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:58.046
<v Peter>Two-millimetre slices, stacked, two-millimetre strips – julienne.

54
00:02:58.046 --> 00:03:00.586
<v Peter>Bundle them, cut across – brunoise.

55
00:03:00.586 --> 00:03:06.100
<v Peter>Three times thicker, six millimetres, and it's bâtonnet – then a small dice.

56
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.178
<v Peter>Julienne for a stir-fry or a salad.

57
00:03:10.178 --> 00:03:13.086
<v Peter>Brunoise for a sauce or a vinaigrette.

58
00:03:13.086 --> 00:03:16.300
<v Peter>Bâtonnet for vegetable sticks – and fries.

59
00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:19.657
<v Peter>The chef's knife rocks.

60
00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:25.800
<v Peter>The tip stays on the board, the heel comes down and glides forward.

61
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.000
<v Peter>No chopping, no lifting.

62
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.660
<v Peter>Rock and glide – that's the rhythm for everything firm.

63
00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:36.743
<v Peter>Soft things – tomato, meat, fish – get a pull instead:

64
00:03:36.743 --> 00:03:41.432
<v Peter>set the blade down and draw it towards you in one long stroke.

65
00:03:41.432 --> 00:03:42.340
<v Peter>Never press.

66
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:48.357
<v Peter>Herbs: fingertips of the free hand on the spine of the blade – never on the edge

67
00:03:48.357 --> 00:03:50.300
<v Peter>– and rock the heel up and down.

68
00:03:52.445 --> 00:03:53.391
<v Peter>The onion.

69
00:03:53.391 --> 00:03:57.743
<v Peter>Halve it through the root and keep the root on

70
00:03:57.743 --> 00:04:02.000
<v Peter>– it holds the layers together while you cut.

71
00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:03.532
<v Peter>Flat side down.

72
00:04:03.532 --> 00:04:07.246
<v Peter>Two horizontal cuts towards the root – not through it.

73
00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:10.479
<v Peter>Then vertical cuts, again stopping at the root.

74
00:04:10.479 --> 00:04:15.225
<v Peter>Now cut across with the claw – and the dice fall apart by themselves.

75
00:04:15.225 --> 00:04:17.220
<v Peter>The root goes in the compost.

76
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.340
<v Peter>Fine cuts, fine dice – that's your brunoise for a sauce.

77
00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.740
<v Peter>Wider cuts, bigger dice for a stew.

78
00:04:27.632 --> 00:04:32.065
<v Peter>Round vegetables: rounds, or on the diagonal for more surface.

79
00:04:32.065 --> 00:04:36.713
<v Peter>Leaves – basil, spinach: stack them, roll them tight, slice thin.

80
00:04:36.713 --> 00:04:38.000
<v Peter>That's chiffonade.

81
00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:39.865
<v Peter>Rounds straight down.

82
00:04:39.865 --> 00:04:43.895
<v Peter>Diagonal at forty-five degrees – bigger pieces, more browning.

83
00:04:43.895 --> 00:04:46.820
<v Peter>And the roll of leaves: one cut, ten ribbons.

84
00:04:50.711 --> 00:04:56.226
<v Peter>The cuts that send people to the ER: cutting towards your own hand.

85
00:04:56.226 --> 00:04:59.930
<v Peter>Avocados, bagels, rolls – never in your hand.

86
00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:01.000
<v Peter>On the board.

87
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:05.080
<v Peter>Never leave a knife in the sink – your hand finds it first.

88
00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:06.779
<v Peter>Never catch a falling knife.

89
00:05:06.779 --> 00:05:10.966
<v Peter>Never force a dull knife through a squash – it's the blade that wins.

90
00:05:10.966 --> 00:05:14.060
<v Peter>And the knife rests on the board, edge facing away.

91
00:05:15.595 --> 00:05:21.134
<v Peter>If it happens: press with a clean cloth, hold the hand up, rinse, plaster.

92
00:05:21.134 --> 00:05:26.000
<v Peter>Deep, gaping, or still bleeding after ten minutes – see a doctor.

93
00:05:26.746 --> 00:05:27.826
<v Peter>Practise slow.

94
00:05:27.826 --> 00:05:31.141
<v Peter>Speed comes from rhythm, not from hurrying.

95
00:05:31.141 --> 00:05:37.000
<v Peter>Ten minutes of carrots a week – in a month, the knife does what you tell it.

96
00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:38.995
<v Peter>So: pinch the blade.

97
00:05:38.995 --> 00:05:42.732
<v Peter>Claw the food – knuckles guide, fingertips tucked.

98
00:05:42.732 --> 00:05:43.853
<v Peter>Flat side down.

99
00:05:43.853 --> 00:05:46.767
<v Peter>Rock, don't chop; pull for soft things.

100
00:05:46.767 --> 00:05:50.504
<v Peter>Julienne two millimetres, cut across for brunoise.

101
00:05:50.504 --> 00:05:52.148
<v Peter>Keep the onion's root.

102
00:05:52.148 --> 00:05:54.540
<v Peter>And never catch a falling knife.

103
00:05:57.681 --> 00:05:58.921
<v Peter>That's it.

104
00:05:58.921 --> 00:06:05.000
<v Peter>Sharp knife, safe hands – see you in the kitchen!
